course: dictionary
lesson: 14745
title: A formal word about laws and ideas
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: «Promulgated» is a formal word. It is the past form of promulgate, so it tells us something happened in the past. When you meet it in a text about laws, rulers or news, look at the words around it to find its meaning.
explain: «The king promulgated a new law about schools in May.» The clues are king and law. A king cannot keep a law secret, because people must know about it. So promulgated means made official by announcing it to the public. Check it: «The king announced a new law about schools in May» still makes sense.
explain: Promulgated can also mean spread an idea widely. «The ideas were promulgated through books and sermons.» Books and sermons carry ideas to many people, so here the ideas were spread far and wide. Both meanings share one thing: making something known to many people.
fact: Promulgate means to make a new law or rule official by announcing it publicly.
fact: Promulgate can also mean to spread an idea or belief widely among people.
fact: The verb forms are promulgate, promulgates, promulgated and promulgating.
fact: «Promulgated» is formal, so you will mostly see it in law, government and history books.
fact: Useful phrases are «promulgate a law», «promulgated by» and «widely promulgated».
exercise: «The council promulgated the new rules so every family in the town could read them.» What does promulgated mean here? | *Officially announced to the public | Hid in a locked box | Broke into small pieces | Forgot about completely
exercise: Which word shows that the action happened in the past? | promulgate | *promulgated | promulgating | promulgates
exercise: «Monks promulgated the teachings in villages far from the city.» What does promulgated suggest? | Kept secret in a cellar | Sung quietly in church | *Spread widely among people | Lost in the post
test: «The king promulgated a new law.» What does promulgated mean? | Torn up and thrown away | *Made official by announcing it publicly | Ignored completely | Written as a poem
test: «The book was widely promulgated.» What does this suggest? | It was given to only one reader | *It was spread widely among many people | It was locked away in a library | It was sold for a very high price
exam: How can you check your meaning of promulgated in a sentence? | Look up every word in a dictionary first | *Swap your meaning in and see if the sentence still makes sense | Skip the whole sentence and move on | Guess the first word you see
